---
import SiteShell from "../../app/providers/site-shell.astro";
import Callout from "../../shared/ui/callout.astro";
import Heading from "../../shared/ui/heading.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../shared/ui/page-header.astro";
import TextLink from "../../shared/ui/text-link.astro";
---

<SiteShell
  title="Why type-tune?"
  description="Understand why moving shared TypeScript declarations needs a deterministic, reviewable workflow."
  withSidebar
>
  <article class="prose">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="Usage"
      title="Why type-tune?"
      lead="Move shared declarations without manually chasing every import and barrel export."
    />

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>The problem</Heading>
      <p>
        Shared types often begin next to the first feature that needs them. Later, API code, UI,
        tests, and application state all import the same declaration from a file that no longer feels
        like the right home.
      </p>
      <p>
        The hard part is not choosing a destination. It is moving the declaration without missing an
        import, breaking a barrel export, or losing a local type dependency. type-tune handles that
        mechanical work while leaving the decision in a reviewable plan.
      </p>
      <Callout>
        Scan output and candidate IDs are deterministic. Optional AI review can annotate a plan, but
        it never controls the AST rewrite path.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>How it differs from related tools</Heading>
      <p>
        type-tune is a focused refactoring tool, not a dead-code detector or a general-purpose
        codemod framework. Its input is a live exported type or interface; its output is a reviewed,
        validated move.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-wrap pixel-frame" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison with related tools">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Tool or category</th>
              <th>Best at</th>
              <th>Typical action</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><TextLink href="https://knip.dev/" external>Knip</TextLink> and dead-code tools</td>
              <td>Finding unused files, dependencies, and exports</td>
              <td>Report or remove code that can disappear</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>IDE refactors</td>
              <td>Moving one declaration you have already chosen</td>
              <td>Perform an interactive, editor-driven move</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Codemod toolkits</td>
              <td>Building custom source transformations</td>
              <td>Provide AST matching and rewrite primitives</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>type-tune</strong></td>
              <td>Finding live shared types that need a better home</td>
              <td>Plan, move, update imports, validate, and roll back on failure</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <Callout>
        Knip removes what is unused. type-tune relocates what is shared. Run cleanup first, then use
        type-tune to reorganize the live types that remain.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>Using type-tune with Knip</Heading>
      <p>
        type-tune may leave a type-only compatibility re-export at the original module path. This
        protects star barrels and consumers outside the selected TypeScript project, but Knip may
        correctly see that old-path export as unused by the files it analyzed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keep the re-export when the old path is still a supported public surface. In that case,
        Knip supports marking the export with <code>@public</code>; see its <TextLink
          href="https://knip.dev/reference/jsdoc-tsdoc-tags#public"
          external>JSDoc and TSDoc tag guidance</TextLink
        >. Remove the compatibility export only after framework builds and external consumers confirm
        that nothing still relies on the old path. Review this case before running Knip auto-fix for
        unused exported types.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>Current scope</Heading>
      <p>
        type-tune analyzes exported type aliases and interfaces in <code>.ts</code> and
        <code>.tsx</code> files loaded from one <code>tsconfig.json</code>. <code>.mts</code> and
        <code>.cts</code> files are not scanned. Run the CLI separately for each package in a
        monorepo. Installation and platform support are in
        <TextLink href="/usage/getting-started">Getting started</TextLink>.
      </p>
      <Callout>
        JavaScript included through <code>allowJs</code>, <code>.svelte</code>, <code>.astro</code>,
        and external package consumers are not scanned or rewritten. <code>.vue</code> files can
        contribute TypeScript referrers from supported script blocks. A move can update those
        import specifiers and then relies on <code>vue-tsc</code> to typecheck the component.
        Compatibility re-exports
        still preserve the original module path for consumers the scan does not rewrite, but you
        should still run framework builds and consumer tests after applying a move.
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteShell>

<style>
  .comparison-wrap {
    margin-block: 1.75rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: var(--frame-step-1);
    --pixel-frame-color: var(--border);
    --pixel-frame-fill: var(--background);
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 42rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  th {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--secondary) 16%, transparent);
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
